 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.1,
   "dur": 4.697,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "How does this cell leave your blood? This wall has no open door."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 5.5,
   "dur": 7.931,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "It's a neutrophil, your commonest white cell, about eight micrometres across. Yet under a sore fingertip, they pile up outside the wall."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 14.3,
   "dur": 10.833,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "Rudolf Virchow taught that pus grew from the inflamed tissue. In eighteen sixty-seven, his assistant Julius Cohnheim watched a living frog's vessels. White cells climbed out."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 25.9,
   "dur": 14.36,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "Where tissue is inflamed, the wall turns sticky, and the cell rolls. Then its grips switch on, and it stops. It crawls to a seam between two wall cells. In a rare inherited condition, those grips are faulty. Cells roll on past."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 42.7,
   "dur": 8.947,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "This one wedges its nucleus into the seam, making its own door. It pours through a gap narrower than itself. Behind it, the seam seals shut."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 55.1,
   "dur": 4.96,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "So pus is mostly white cells from your blood. Each one let itself out."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 61.1,
   "dur": 5.747,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "Meanwhile, as you watched, your bone marrow made new neutrophils. More than ten million."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 67.9,
   "dur": 4.212,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next: the spark. What sets off nearly every heartbeat you have?"
  }
 ]
}